Z||000007||Ken Wittlief||06-02-2001||10:06 AM||||Is your company moving you? New company relocating you?<P>  if you are using a big moving company to relocate, some will put a car right in the back of the truck as long as you drain the gas out first.<P>  And most companies, if you are being relocated, will pay to have one car moved.<P>  If they put it in the moving van they charge by the pound - I dont know what the going rates are but it might be cheaper that paying a car transporter.<P>  Anoter option is to find someplace you can park it for 6 months or a year, and then go back and get it when you have the chance - maybe not so easy to find in California, but its a thought.||||reg||1
Z||000008||jlruiz67||06-02-2001||07:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Urchin:<BR><B>I'm not sure about transporting it, but I just thought I'd say congratulations!<BR>And glad to see you didn't have to sell the car.  My dad had a '69 chevelle and had to sell it when he married to pay for the wedding and house etc.   Good luck<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thank you all for your help but I wont be shipping the car to Chicago anymore. Shipping seems to be anywhere from $600-$1200 the value of the car is what? Some where between $1500 and $2500, the car has to be shipped back in a year. Doing the math I can't see my self, shipping the car, and I could buy another one in Chicago or in the east coast for cheap if I decide to stay there. So for now I think I'm going to sell my 87GT Med Red, 210,000miles running strong. I'll be posting it on the mall soon.<P>Thank you all<BR>||||reg||9
